{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "During initialization, the policy localized visible colored cubes differently because the rule specified cover geometry but not the cube landmark. During exchanges it sometimes substituted another target's stored coordinate for a measurement; after grasp closure it sometimes treated finger occlusion as preventing all localization. These cause PREDICTABILITY failures even where the next waypoint is within tolerance. Keep the ordered list of last reliably measured target central-axis [x,y] coordinates. Specify initialization from the center of each cube's horizontal top face at approximately z=0.03 m, using calibrated views rather than the whole silhouette or a table-plane projection. For covers, distinguish observed support from mere cessation of image motion: a visible lower boundary consistent with table contact and full-face geometry consistent with the resting cover height can establish support at an exchange endpoint; unchanged supported geometry can carry that evidence forward. Closure around a previously supported cover does not invalidate its resting height when the clip shows no lift or tipping. Visible opposing outer edges or reconstructible outer corners in the wrist view can then establish the center despite finger occlusion. Update an uncompleted entry when this measurement establishes displacement; retain it when measurements agree within visual uncertainty. For example, a pre-lift measurement may change [[0.021,-0.125],[0.025,0.043]] to [[0.019,-0.124],[0.025,0.043]]. Do not obtain an exchanged target's coordinate merely by copying a former occupant's stored location. Reaudit endpoint updates and anchor agreement together: if endpoint support or localization is unresolved, retain the last reliable coordinates and do not annotate a new localization anchor. Preserve the existing successful-lift freeze and causal identity-tracking requirements.",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "At the first unchanged front frame after a reliable anchor, policies retained the old anchor as well as the new frame because the new frame lacked an independent wrist measurement. At grasp closure they retained the entire approach history although the current views constrained the still-supported cover. Annotated singleton anchors at exchange endpoints also require consistency with the target measurements, not merely a clear configuration. These are PREDICTABILITY failures. Keep the ordered trajectory list and latest synchronized-anchor compression rule, but explicitly allow a newly shown frame to inherit established localization and support when chronological comparison establishes unchanged target geometry and unambiguous correspondence. No new wrist image or changed coordinate is required. This inheritance requires positive visible continuity, not merely an absence of a reliable new measurement; it cannot bridge an exchange, an unresolved elevation change, or an invisible target. At a closed but not yet lifted grasp, combine the current wrist view's exposed outer geometry with front-view support and correspondence evidence; finger occlusion alone does not disqualify an anchor. Only remaining requested targets must qualify. For example, when E55 inherits a valid supported configuration and later frames show motion, write [E55,E71,E84]; when the current grasp frame independently qualifies for every remaining target, write [E287]. If an exchange endpoint's coordinates remain stale because localization is unresolved, retain the earlier anchor and intervening frames rather than compressing to that endpoint. Audit annotations for this consistency.",
      "value_type": "keyframe"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_locations",
      "description": "Initialization failures arose from using an unspecified cube landmark; exchange failures arose from confusing identity arrival with metric localization; grasp-time failures arose from rejecting partially occluded but measurable geometry. These break PREDICTABILITY. Keep requested-order central-axis coordinates. Initialize each visible cube from its horizontal top-face center at approximately z=0.03 m, checking agreement between calibrated views where available; do not use the silhouette center or project the elevated face onto the table. For covers, establish resting height from visible table-contact boundaries and face geometry consistent with support, or carry forward previously established support through positively observed unchanged geometry. A clear top center, an accepted robot waypoint, or momentary stationarity alone is insufficient. Trace exchanges before measuring endpoints. Never substitute another entry's old coordinate simply because the tracked target appears to occupy its former region. After grasp closure, use continuity from the supported approach and the absence of observed lift or tipping to retain the resting-height interpretation. Inspect the wrist image for opposing outer edges or reconstructible outer corners, excluding the fingers and inset panels; partial occlusion does not prevent measurement when these constrain the center. Update uncompleted entries for resolved displacement, retain values when the measurement agrees within uncertainty, and freeze only after the successful lift is observed. For example, a grasp-time update can yield [[0.019,-0.124],[0.025,0.043]]. If support, correspondence, or center remains unresolved, retain the previous coordinate and preserve the trajectory needed to interpret it."
    },
    {
      "name": "tracking_frame",
      "description": "Policies unnecessarily retained a preceding anchor when a new front frame showed the same reliably supported targets, and retained approach histories at closed grasps whose current views still localized all remaining targets. Conversely, a clear exchange endpoint must not become an anchor while its target coordinates remain unresolved. These are PREDICTABILITY failures. Append new labels chronologically, then test for the latest synchronized anchor. A new frame may inherit localization and support from the preceding reliable evidence when comparison of the retained frames and current clip positively establishes unchanged visible target geometry and correspondence. It need not have its own wrist view or produce different coordinates. Do not inherit through an intervening exchange, unresolved height change, or invisibility. Once targets move, use fresh reliable localization before making another anchor. At grasp closure, use the current wrist and front views together: still-supported height and exposed opposing outer geometry can qualify the grasped target despite fingers hiding part of it; other remaining targets must also be localized. Completed targets need not qualify. Thus an unchanged first frame followed by motion may produce [E55,E71,E84], while a sufficiently visible current grasp may produce [E287]. If no frame qualifies, retain the earlier anchor and the full subsequent trajectory. Never discard the trajectory merely because the endpoint looks settled or because a stale coordinate was carried unchanged."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "Do not add episode-specific coordinates, named-frame exceptions, or an action correction for these reports. The reported lift waypoints generally remained within the required positional tolerance, and the existing action rules already permit visual centering and require tracking before selection. The substantive failures concern memory predictability. Do not accept the claim that successful identity tracking alone permits swapping stored coordinate entries, nor the inconsistent combination of unresolved endpoint localization with a new singleton localization anchor. If localization truly remains unresolved, both the coordinates and the earlier trajectory evidence must be retained; if endpoint geometry establishes a fresh measurement, update the coordinates consistently before compressing. Likewise, do not declare every exchange endpoint supported merely because it resembles a prior arrangement, and do not declare every closed grasp measurable merely because the gripper has closed. The proposed support and partial-occlusion clarifications apply only when the currently available evidence satisfies them. Existing milestones and pickup-count semantics need no change."
}

